# ALL.wantrc — DECLARED exit codes for entries whose CORRECT run exits nonzero.
# Format: name<TAB>rc   ·   blank lines and # comments ignored   ·   rc=0 is the default and is REFUSED here.
# See tests/prolog/ALL.wantrc for the convention this file follows.
#
# The expected rc is the ORACLE's (Lon 2026-11-05: the expected result is the oracle's exit code, stdout and stderr), measured with sbl -bf through
# util_oracle.sh on the scrip --transpile text of each entry on SCRIP 5217bd1e8, 2026-11-05. The earlier rc 1 lines were declared from SCRIP's old
# one-voice exit 1, never from the oracle.
#
# simple_output_25 (origin ladder__rung07_refuse_field_of_nonrecord) declares nothing: calling a DATA()-generated field accessor (x(...)) on a value
# that is not a `point` record is a runtime error, SCRIP prints "before" to stdout and the diagnostic to STDERR only, and the oracle exits 0.
# alt_replace_3 (origin ladder__rung09_refuse_alt_as_number) declares nothing: `1 | 2 | 3` outside a scan/pattern context is a genuine SNOBOL4
# PATTERN value (TT_ALT lowers to the native pattern-alternation operator), so comparing it numerically (`= 2`) is "Error 101: eq first argument is
# not numeric"; stdout "before\n", and the oracle exits 0 (a fatal at the default &CODE exits 0, after &CODE = 7 it exits 7).
#
# len_capture_1 (origin ladder__rung10_refuse_len_wrong_type): LEN() takes an integer length, not a string -- LEN("abc") is "Error 120: len argument
# is not integer or expression"; stdout "before\n". The ORACLE exits 231, and 231 even with &CODE = 7, so it is not &CODE. SCRIP exits 0 until the
# runtime's error exit carries the oracle's code (row rebus-rebrungs-four-programs-exit-0-where-spitbol-exits-1-...), so this entry reads RED honestly.
len_capture_1	231
#
# len_1 (origin ladder__rung11_refuse_bad_pattern_arg): same LEN()-wrong-type runtime error as len_capture_1 above, this time inside a replace-form
# scan (`s ? LEN("abc") <- "X"`) on a plain variable subject -- Error 120, stdout "before\n", the oracle exits 231, SCRIP 0 until the gap closes.
# Deliberately NOT the "replacement subject must be a plain variable" FATAL (a lowering-time abort on a literal subject, e.g. `"hello" ? "l" <- "L"`):
# that case produces truly zero stdout bytes and hits the same unrepresentable-in-the-ladder trap as rung06's parse-error refuse attempt (see task LEDGER).
len_1	231
